#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060507} :activity/id #uuid "cfab7801-e5d9-5cc5-82dd-9b1700800489" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049773} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060507 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060889 :tutorial/content "The following two identities are known as the **sum and difference formulas for sine**:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}(x+y) & =\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}y+\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{sin}y \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}(x-y) & =\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}y-\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{sin}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe'll prove these results at the end of the lesson.\n\nThese identities can be used to calculate the sine of an angle which is the sum or difference of two special angles. \n\nFor example, we can use the sum rule to find the exact value of $\\sin(75^\\circ).$ We start by writing $75^\\circ$ as a sum of special angles:\n\n$$\n75^\\circ = {\\color{blue}45^\\circ} + {\\color{red}30^\\circ}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula for sine as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}{75}^{∘} & =\\operatorname{sin}({45}^{∘}+{30}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}{45}^{∘}\\operatorname{cos}{30}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cos}{45}^{∘}\\operatorname{sin}{30}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}+\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\sin 75^\\circ =   \\dfrac {\\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2}} 4.\n$$\n\nLet's see another example." :tutorial/id #uuid "a8b2cde2-4c10-51d6-b6f7-3b2a941a1d98" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2559 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "1a9ccd10-0e50-5e86-b3d2-338000cdb1c8" :step/math-academy-id 11728 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060509}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060508 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059662 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059493 :question/id #uuid "4cd24c10-1838-5f4b-ac88-cfbfc2ec4c21" :question/math-academy-id "e-3518" :question/problem "Express $\\sin\\left(x - 30^{\\circ} \\right )$ in terms of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin(u-v) = \\sin u\\cos v - \\cos u\\sin v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v=30^{\\circ},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{30}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "69e91ce7-259a-5a11-bb79-7e6ad7ea86d4" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701824} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060120} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060352} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233843} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233848} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233849} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272252} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272253} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272254} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272255} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272256} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292752} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304991} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304992} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304993} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304994} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701658} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701660} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701661} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701793} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701797} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701807} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701813} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701817} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705267} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705269} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705309} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705311} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705312} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705320}] :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions in Degrees Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"} :step/id #uuid "08e66865-9eab-589a-a7af-77b1058ad01d" :step/math-academy-id 11729 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060759}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060509 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059663 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059494 :question/id #uuid "7341638b-3c0f-5c10-8b7d-a2c313607bed" :question/math-academy-id "e-3519" :question/problem "Find the exact value of $\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}\\right)$ without using a calculator.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\pi}{12} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} - \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}.\n$$*" :question/worked-solution "Recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin(x-y) = \\sin x\\cos y - \\cos x\\sin y\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for sine with $x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$ and $y = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}-\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "213eda96-c41d-5361-9da9-8349617014f1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049923} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050848}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701936} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060002} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060103} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233846} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233847} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253530} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292748} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335834} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335835} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701916} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701917} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701919} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701924} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701934} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705137} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705143} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705144} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705148} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705190} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705211} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705213} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705214} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705254} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705257} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705264}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Values Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"} :step/id #uuid "a3de70e8-719f-5346-882f-897c95f91dfb" :step/math-academy-id 11732 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060508}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060759 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059820 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059627 :question/id #uuid "0ce71d86-7470-5d53-ac94-20b473889dee" :question/math-academy-id "e-9983" :question/problem "Express $\\sin\\left(x + \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\right)$ in terms of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin(u+v) = \\sin{u}\\cos{v} + \\cos{u}\\sin{v}\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for sine with $u = x$ and $v = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b12a7b7-1df3-5203-934e-8820604de3de" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701968} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060109} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060281} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233451} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233585} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233844} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233850} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233851} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272257} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272258} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272322} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272323} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304795} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304996} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304998} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304999} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335836} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701780} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701791} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701792} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701941} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701956} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701957} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701965} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701967} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705162} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705171} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705180} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705187} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705219} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705221} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705238}] :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions in Radians Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"} :step/id #uuid "23953749-c368-59ef-b666-9cb9c2e9f984" :step/math-academy-id 37601 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060806}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060806 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060866 :tutorial/content "Let's now prove the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin(A+B) = \\sin A\\cos B + \\cos A\\sin B\n$$\n\nTo start with, we need to use the *difference* formula for *cosine*:\n\n$$\n\\cos(A-B) = \\cos A\\cos B + \\sin A\\sin B\n$$\n\nIf you haven't seen this formula yet, don't worry. You'll learn more about it, including how it's proved using the unit circle, in a separate lesson.\n\nNow, if we replace $A$ with $\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} - A\\right)$ in the difference formula for cosine, we get\n\n$$\n\\cos\\left(\\dfrac\\pi2 - A-B\\right) = \\cos \\left(\\dfrac\\pi2 - A\\right)\\cos B + \\sin\\left(\\dfrac\\pi2 - A\\right)\\sin B\n$$\n\ni.e.\n\n$$\n\\cos\\left(\\dfrac\\pi2 - (A+B)\\right) = \\cos \\left(\\dfrac\\pi2 - A\\right)\\cos B + \\sin\\left(\\dfrac\\pi2 - A\\right)\\sin B.\n$$\n\nNext, using the cofunction identities\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-(A+B)\\right) & =\\operatorname{sin}(A+B) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-A\\right) & =\\operatorname{sin}A \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-A\\right) & =\\operatorname{cos}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe identity above reduces to\n\n$$\n\\sin(A+B) = \\sin A\\cos B + \\cos A\\sin B\n$$\n\nas required." :tutorial/id #uuid "27cf015f-7be2-50f5-aed3-a5dc1f2cb26e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13284 :tutorial/title "Proving the Sum Formula"} :step/id #uuid "7ca2f832-c9fe-56ac-bb63-b51f4bfb5a79" :step/math-academy-id 461995 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060807}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060807 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060867 :tutorial/content "We have shown how to derive the sum formula\n\n$$\n\\sin(A+B) = \\sin A\\cos B + \\cos A\\sin B.\n$$\n\nWe can derive the difference formula for sine by simply replacing $B$ with $-B,$ and using the evenness/oddness identities for cosine and sine, respectively:\n\n$$\n\\cos(-B) = \\cos B, \\qquad \\sin(-B) = -\\sin B\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into our sum formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}(A+(-B))=\\operatorname{sin}A\\operatorname{cos}(-B)+\\operatorname{cos}A\\operatorname{sin}(-B)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n\\sin(A-B) = \\sin A\\cos B - \\cos A\\sin B\n$$\n\nas required." :tutorial/id #uuid "93e513c3-90cf-588d-84a3-ed0b82e82401" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13285 :tutorial/title "Proving the Difference Formula"} :step/id #uuid "abef5a7a-78f9-5491-81b5-f9c42898d085" :step/math-academy-id 461996}] :activity/title "The Sum and Difference Formulas for Sine" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059945}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060523} :activity/id #uuid "6247c421-7779-51f5-a4bd-3cde04307793" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048362} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060523 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060892 :tutorial/content "Recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin (x+y) = \\sin x \\cos y + \\cos x \\sin y\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting $y= {\\color{blue}x}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}(x+x) & =\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x+\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{sin}x \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}2x & =\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x+\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}2x & =2\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equation above is called the **double-angle formula for sine**:\n\n$$\n\\sin 2x = 2 \\sin x \\cos x\n$$\n\nAs we will see, this formula has many applications. For instance, it allows us to simplify some trigonometric expressions and find the exact value of sine for some non-special angles.\n\nIt's important to realize that this is an identity, *not* an equation, which means that it is true for *all* values of $x.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d14ca467-0ecd-5ac3-ab88-9bc2182a02ce" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2609 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "31a9aaf3-ced6-5bd9-a812-e8088d84003a" :step/math-academy-id 12230 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060524}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060524 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059671 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059499 :question/id #uuid "e95210c5-adb4-519e-bbec-e240dac32fd8" :question/math-academy-id "e-3614" :question/problem "Write $2\\sin{15^\\circ} \\cos{15^\\circ}$ as a single trigonometric ratio." :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\sin{x}\\cos{x} = \\sin 2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=15^\\circ$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡{15}^{∘}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{15}^{∘} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot {15}^{∘})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "143766e0-0ef5-5caf-b699-d394d4a707e9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049773}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696204} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060399} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060402} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233278} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234047} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253624} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290560} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341901} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341926} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345251} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345252} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186695676} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186695678} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186695694} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696171} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696182} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696190} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696192} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696194} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696225} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696227} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696234} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696247} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696252} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696264}] :knowledge-point/title "Using the Double-Angle Formula to Simplify Products"} :step/id #uuid "2697bac8-84d0-5fbd-ad5d-f33c14b3b3da" :step/math-academy-id 12231 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060526}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060525 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059672 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059500 :question/id #uuid "35535b08-b3d8-5037-a821-6815577169d2" :question/math-academy-id "e-3618" :question/problem "Without using a calculator, find the exact value of $$\n\\displaystyle \\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right)}\\cos{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right)}\\cos{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}\\right)}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\sin{x}\\cos{x} = \\sin{2x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n2\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right)\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right) = \\sin\\left(2\\cdot \\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n2\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right)\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right) = \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}\\right) .\n$$\n\nNow, we can rewrite the given expression using the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{24}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{24}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left[2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{24}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{24}\\right)\\right]\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the double-angle formula once more with $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{12},$ we can further simplify the above expression: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\left[2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)\\right] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\left[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)\\right] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right)}\\cos{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right)}\\cos{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}\\right)} = \\dfrac{1}{8}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d820aa9f-7c02-53b5-9b09-c25ee59dbdaf" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696444} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060239} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060243} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233282} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233283} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253642} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253643} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290559} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290562} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290577} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302344} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323805} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323806} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345243} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345244} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696008} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696009} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696011} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696414} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696423} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696428} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696434} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696466} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696472} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696479} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696484}] :knowledge-point/title "Using the Double-Angle Formula Multiple Times"} :step/id #uuid "758d1a15-deaf-5a7d-a2b3-ab5cdc948bda" :step/math-academy-id 12270} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060526 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059673 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059501 :question/id #uuid "b03496ac-c3b2-5d65-a3e3-2a33ea0cb862" :question/math-academy-id "e-3619" :question/problem "Simplify the expression $\\dfrac{\\sin 25^\\circ}{\\sin 50^\\circ}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n\\sin{2x} = 2\\sin{x}\\cos{x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=25^\\circ$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left(2\\cdot 25^\\circ\\right) = 2\\sin 25^\\circ \\cos 25^\\circ\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\sin 50^\\circ = 2\\sin 25^\\circ \\cos 25^\\circ.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can simplify the given expression by rewriting the ${\\color{blue} \\sin 50^\\circ}$ in the denominator as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{25}^{∘}}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{50}^{∘}} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{25}^{∘}}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡{25}^{∘}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{25}^{∘}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{25}^{∘}}}{2\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{25}^{∘}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{25}^{∘}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡{25}^{∘}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡{25}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f9683d9-05b9-5a42-9f81-0170a15dfac1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050928}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696372} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060241} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060242} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233279} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253627} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253628} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253629} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253630} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253632} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253633} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290558} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335849} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335850} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335851} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335852} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696346} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696347} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696353} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696356} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696369} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696446} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696456} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696490} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696500}] :knowledge-point/title "Using the Double-Angle Formula to Simplify Ratios"} :step/id #uuid "2e2b09a9-557f-5799-9a72-622f4f3713d3" :step/math-academy-id 12275 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060527}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060527 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059674 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059502 :question/id #uuid "539b5b86-7b31-52c9-be04-e80d6799b68a" :question/math-academy-id "e-3623" :question/problem "Without using a calculator, find the exact value of $$\n\\displaystyle 3\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}\\right)}\\cos{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}\\right)}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\sin{x}\\cos{x} = \\sin 2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\dfrac {\\pi}{8}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n2\\sin \\left(\\dfrac {\\pi} {8} \\right) \\cos \\left(\\dfrac {\\pi} {8} \\right) = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the above by $\\dfrac 1 2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n3 \\, {\\color{black}\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}\\right)}\\cos{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}\\right)} } = 3 \\left( \\color{black} \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\right) = \\dfrac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c898bbf-004c-5905-a0a0-761adefc8bd8" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696408} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060249} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060398} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233280} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233281} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253634} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253636} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290576} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292685} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302330} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302332} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302346} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302347} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345239} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345240} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696386} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696392} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696405} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696501} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696509}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Value of a Trigonometric Expression Using the Double-Angle Formula"} :step/id #uuid "6c628c14-aae8-5b84-9c12-e5b22b1018e6" :step/math-academy-id 12302 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060525}}] :activity/title "The Double-Angle Formula for Sine" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059946}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060512} :activity/id #uuid "17339bb9-1659-5064-a793-2d84ac6e8242" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048319} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060512 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060891 :tutorial/content "The following two identities are known as the **sum and difference formulas for cosine**:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}(x\\,+\\,y) & =\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{cos}y\\,\\,-\\,\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{sin}y \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}(x\\,-\\,y) & =\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{cos}y\\,\\,+\\,\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{sin}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe'll prove these results at the end of the lesson.\n\nNotice that the signs are somewhat counterintuitive. The sum formula for cosine contains a minus sign $({\\color{red}{-}}),$ while the difference formula contains a plus sign $({\\color{blue}{+}}).$ \n\nThese identities can help calculate the cosine of an angle that can be expressed as the sum or difference of two special angles.\n\nFor example, we can use the sum formula to find the exact value of $\\cos(105^\\circ).$ First, we write\n\n$$\n105^\\circ = {\\color{blue}45^\\circ} + {\\color{red}60^\\circ}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula for cosine, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}{105}^{∘} & =\\operatorname{cos}({45}^{∘}+{60}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}{45}^{∘}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}60-\\operatorname{sin}{45}^{∘}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}60 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{6}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a9114a20-3220-5a44-b0ed-4e405d4317a4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2563 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "2afe3115-494d-5055-8e96-efb5e53aeb5e" :step/math-academy-id 11766 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060514}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060513 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059665 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059496 :question/id #uuid "1d8679d3-8922-5471-811f-60c8c41c853a" :question/math-academy-id "e-3522" :question/problem "Express $\\cos\\left( x - \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right)$ in terms of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos(u - v) = \\cos u \\cos v + \\sin u \\sin v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for cosine with $u = x$ and $v = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot 0+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "831f9aa0-099e-5e0c-8a9d-c4828b629f64" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186714898} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060104} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060114} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233455} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234046} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234178} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253583} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272275} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272276} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272277} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292741} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292742} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305045} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305046} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305047} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305048} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305049} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305050} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305051} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186714557} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186714558} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186714569} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186714885} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186714886} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186714890} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186714893} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186714895} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186714899} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186714906} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186714911} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186714921}] :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions in Radians Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"} :step/id #uuid "53346d2f-5b1b-51d4-a6bb-f60bd1e5c54e" :step/math-academy-id 11767 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060804}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060514 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059666 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059497 :question/id #uuid "bdb79801-7c8f-5b39-8730-85dcc3d9cdd0" :question/math-academy-id "e-3523" :question/problem "Find the exact value of $\\cos \\left( -\\dfrac{\\pi}{12} \\right).$\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle -\\dfrac{\\pi}{12} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} - \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}.\n$$*" :question/worked-solution "Recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos(x - y) = \\cos{x}\\cos{y} + \\sin{x}\\sin{y}\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for cosine with $x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ and $y = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}+\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "283415d8-47d7-5d42-8836-0c0b0c4efc85" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049923} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050848}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715295} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060115} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060357} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233270} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233852} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233853} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233854} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233855} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233856} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234045} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234179} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234180} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253581} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272269} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284690} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292738} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305039} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186714819} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186714820} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186714827} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715276} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715280} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715282} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715288} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715293} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715336} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715337} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715339} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715346} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715397}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Values Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"} :step/id #uuid "a8c43958-8da7-5043-96a5-0deaa552b575" :step/math-academy-id 11772 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060760}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060760 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059821 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059628 :question/id #uuid "b643e820-52c3-5c08-a099-aecbed7b2b2a" :question/math-academy-id "e-9991" :question/problem "Express $\\cos(x - 60^\\circ)$ in terms of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos(u - v) = \\cos{u}\\cos{v} + \\sin{u}\\sin{v}\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for cosine with $u = x$ and $v = 60^\\circ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-{60}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡{60}^{∘}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6a58962-cb06-519a-8c64-0dfa3b9c14d7" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715675} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060354} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060355} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233707} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233857} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253582} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272270} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272271} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272272} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272273} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272274} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272331} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305040} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305041} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305042} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305043} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305044} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335842} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715259} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715266} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715271} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715650} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715659} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715662} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715668} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715669} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715680} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715689} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715693} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715695}] :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions in Degrees Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"} :step/id #uuid "8279c645-73d9-562a-a923-1077bbc0cf0e" :step/math-academy-id 37636 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060513}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060804 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060864 :tutorial/content "Let's now prove the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos(A-B) = \\cos A\\cos B + \\sin A\\sin B\n$$\n\nConsider two points $P$ and $Q$ on the unit circle whose central angles have measures $A$ and $B,$ respectively, as shown below.\n\n![](images/5e/5ed2cf9e528e7ec6762875f3dfaf211aba656e6b39025109315539a7dd4b013a.png)\n\nSince $P$ and $Q$ lie on the unit circle, their coordinates are\n\n$$\nP(\\cos A, \\sin A), \\qquad Q(\\cos B, \\sin B).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the squared distance $PQ^2$ between $P$ and $Q$ can be found using the distance formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P{Q}^{2} & ={(\\operatorname{cos}A-\\operatorname{cos}B)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}A-\\operatorname{sin}B)}^{2} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}A-2\\operatorname{cos}A\\operatorname{cos}B+{cos}^{2}B+{sin}^{2}A-2\\operatorname{sin}A\\operatorname{sin}B+{sin}^{2}B \\\\  & =({cos}^{2}A+{sin}^{2}A)+({cos}^{2}B+{sin}^{2}B)-2(\\operatorname{cos}A\\operatorname{cos}B+\\operatorname{sin}A\\operatorname{sin}B)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\cos^2 B + \\sin^2 B = \\cos^2 A + \\sin^2 A = 1\n$$\n\nfor all $A$ and $B$ by the Pythagorean identity, our expression for $PQ^2$ simplifies as\n\n$$\nPQ^2 = 1+1 - 2(\\cos A\\cos B + \\sin A\\sin B)\n$$\n\ni.e., \n\n$$\nPQ^2 = 2 - 2(\\cos A\\cos B + \\sin A\\sin B).\n$$\n\nWe can also calculate $PQ^2$ by applying the law of cosines to $\\triangle POQ{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P{Q}^{2}={1}^{2}+{1}^{2}-2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}(A-B)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\nPQ^2 = 2 - 2\\cos(A-B).\n$$\n\nEquating the two expressions for $PQ^2,$ we get\n\n$$\n2 - 2(\\cos A\\cos B + \\sin A\\sin B) = 2 - 2\\cos(A-B)\n$$\n\n \n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\cos(A-B) = \\cos A\\cos B + \\sin A\\sin B\n$$\n\nas required." :tutorial/id #uuid "98298911-e456-5b6c-92f0-315fe2bfca4a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13282 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Difference Formula"} :step/id #uuid "00e554fc-e6f5-5f65-bed6-576c4f6d26f7" :step/math-academy-id 461993 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060805}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060805 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060865 :tutorial/content "We have shown how to derive the difference formula\n\n$$\n\\cos(A-B) = \\cos A\\cos B + \\sin A\\sin B.\n$$\n\nWe can derive the sum formula for cosine by simply replacing $B$ with $-B,$ and using the evenness/oddness identities for cosine and sine, respectively:\n\n$$\n\\cos(-B) = \\cos B, \\qquad \\sin(-B) = -\\sin B\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into our difference formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}(A-(-B))=\\operatorname{cos}A\\operatorname{cos}(-B)+\\operatorname{sin}A\\operatorname{sin}(-B)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n\\cos(A+B) = \\cos A\\cos B - \\sin A\\sin B.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "67134b4c-9f78-59b0-b0e7-42049c393c9c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13283 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Sum Formula"} :step/id #uuid "5e38ce17-dfbd-58ac-95c8-c7020d6e1b3c" :step/math-academy-id 461994}] :activity/title "The Sum and Difference Formulas for Cosine" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059948}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088478} :activity/id #uuid "db2aa40b-702a-52ed-baea-d499f50ff54a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048703} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088478 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088880 :tutorial/content "Recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos(x+y) = \\cos x \\cos y - \\sin x \\sin y\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $y={\\color{blue}x}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\cos 2x = \\cos^2 {x}  - \\sin^2 {x}.\n$$\n\nThis is called the **double-angle formula for cosine**." :tutorial/id #uuid "8597693a-d6bd-59b2-bc3a-6922057a55be" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2638 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "509b8966-516b-5a1a-850b-bc2e1fe1df97" :step/math-academy-id 12374 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088482}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088482 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087669 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087477 :question/id #uuid "7826cd52-8603-51ff-902e-c7764639dccd" :question/math-academy-id "e-3666" :question/problem "Express $$\n\\displaystyle \\cos^2{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right)} - \\sin^2{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right)}\n$$ as a single trigonometric ratio." :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos^2 x - \\sin^2 x = \\cos 2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{24}\\right)-{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{24}\\right) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{24}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ec37c5e-8a40-58a7-8af3-4457cd5da43f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048319}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698976} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088094} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088294} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256392} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256393} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256394} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290550} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345214} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345258} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186598584} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186598626} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186660919} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698875} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698882} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698883} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698945} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698947} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698960} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698975} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703439} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703441} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703444} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703447} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703467} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703470} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703480} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703487}] :knowledge-point/title "Using the Double-Angle Formula to Simplify Expressions"} :step/id #uuid "9a6deb20-15ba-561d-abcd-d2927bc14f03" :step/math-academy-id 12391 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088483}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088483 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088881 :tutorial/content "It's possible to write the double angle formula for cosine in two alternative forms.\n\nFrom the Pythagorean identity $\\sin^2{x}+\\cos^2{x}=1,$ we obtain $\\cos^2{x}=1-\\sin^2{x}.$\nSubstituting this into the double-angle formula for cosine, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x & ={\\cos }^{2}⁡x-{\\sin }^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =(1-{\\sin }^{2}⁡x)-{\\sin }^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =1-2{\\sin }^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives an alternative form of the double-angle formula for cosine. We'll restate it below:\n\n$$\n\\cos{2x} =1 - 2\\sin^2{x}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, from the same Pythagorean identity, we obtain that $\\sin^2{x}=1-\\cos^2{x}.$ Substituting into the double-angle formula for cosine, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x & ={\\cos }^{2}⁡x-{\\sin }^{2}⁡x \\\\  & ={\\cos }^{2}⁡x-(1-{\\cos }^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =2{\\cos }^{2}⁡x-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is another alternative form of the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos{2x} =2\\cos^2{x}-1\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5da93916-34c4-5c5a-806c-82bae4dc1e02" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2639 :tutorial/title "Alternative Forms of the Double-Angle Formula for Cosine"} :step/id #uuid "2058340e-7371-58c9-98d0-c006925b2662" :step/math-academy-id 12394 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088484}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088484 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087670 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087478 :question/id #uuid "2b9dc462-ec26-5762-aa64-d9e8f16112c9" :question/math-academy-id "e-3667" :question/problem "Express $2\\cos^2{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)}-1$ as a single trigonometric ratio." :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n2 \\cos^2 x - 1 = \\cos 2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)-1 & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12ff17ae-9319-5498-a5e0-1d49ddbef50a" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703575} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088095} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088339} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256396} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256397} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256398} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290546} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341956} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344970} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345262} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345263} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345265} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345266} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186598566} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186600356} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186600406} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698902} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698903} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698910} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698993} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698996} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698998} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186699002} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186699008} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703559} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703560} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703565} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703570}] :knowledge-point/title "Using Alternative Forms of Double-Angle Formula to Simplify Expressions"} :step/id #uuid "e9090058-9d48-50de-beb0-3295dfa48fcc" :step/math-academy-id 12395 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088485}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088485 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087671 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087479 :question/id #uuid "e617aaf2-1232-57cc-8644-2467effda030" :question/math-academy-id "e-3668" :question/problem "Without using a calculator, find the exact value of $\\cos^2\\left( 22.5^\\circ\\right) - \\sin^2\\left( 22.5^\\circ\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos^2 x - \\sin^2 x = \\cos 2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={22.5^\\circ}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\cos }^{2}⁡({22.5}^{∘})-{\\sin }^{2}⁡({22.5}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot {22.5}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡{45}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2902b205-4508-55c6-9a68-f57d3afc9eab" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186699271} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088096} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088111} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256399} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256400} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256401} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256402} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292878} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304985} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304986} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304987} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304988} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304989} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304990} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344972} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344977} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186699252} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186699259} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186699260} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186699263} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186699269} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186702810}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Values of Trigonometric Expressions Using the Double-Angle Formula"} :step/id #uuid "e9a41c14-5841-5d8f-a075-7bde19b9c6d6" :step/math-academy-id 12399}] :activity/title "The Double-Angle Formula for Cosine" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087908}]}
